7592186446808 1078 #edb/ref 17592186446754 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446808 1079 #uuid "d16c3309-85b6-5b20-bebc-f505a6812eb7" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446802 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446809 1069 "q-99235" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446809 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum sum of the square roots of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $27.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446809 1072 #uuid "1eb20554-7ec2-5620-86b4-093c81339236" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446809 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}=2\\lambda x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}=2\\lambda y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-3/2}=4\\lambda  \\\\ {y}^{-3/2}=4\\lambda  \\\\ {z}^{-3/2}=4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,1,1) & =\\sqrt{5}+1+1 \\\\  & =2+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & <f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446805 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446810 1069 "q-99203" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446810 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum value of the sum of the sines of angle measures of a triangle." 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446810 1072 #uuid "302ed073-7778-5ce1-8530-7759debd7de4" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446810 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cosine function is one-to-one on the interval $(0,\\pi ),$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446804 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446811 1069 "q-99270" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446811 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3y$ given that ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 14.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446811 1072 #uuid "4c584a36-c1bc-53fc-9ad2-f5f8790f1c21" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446811 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=14.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=14\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ -3=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Substituting $x=-2y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=14,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-2y=∓2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-7\\sqrt{2}$ | $7\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-7\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446808 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446812 1069 "q-162796" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446812 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of the cosines of three non-negative angles whose sum is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446812 1072 #uuid "71aa525f-c293-54c2-9046-61ecb78eb1e5" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446812 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nWe also have the constraints $0\\le x,y,z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x+x+x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446803 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446813 1069 "q-162778" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446813 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446813 1072 #uuid "74a32659-ab2b-5195-9c2f-351117b323e4" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446813 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446806 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446814 1069 "q-162794" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446814 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the minimum value of the sum of the cosines of three obtuse angles whose sum is $2\\pi .$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446814 1072 #uuid "7a65655b-2417-5001-b909-d38270394c2a" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446814 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the angles are obtuse, we also have the constraints $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x,y,z<\\pi .$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=2\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ),$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =2\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =2\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & >f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446807 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446815 1069 "q-162800" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446815 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+y\\le 2.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446815 1072 #uuid "7c5088ac-9a02-5898-afc8-f9931b4dcdf9" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446815 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+y=2.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives us the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -4y=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{4},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =2\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{17}{8}.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446799 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446816 1069 "q-99269" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446816 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1$ is $(0,0).$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446816 1072 #uuid "844fd53e-ecbf-5f52-be0b-e7df67880b9a" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446816 1074 "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1+\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 1).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 1,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 1),\\,(\\pm 1,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446800 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446817 1069 "q-162781" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446817 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446817 1072 #uuid "a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446817 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ -4=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-2\\,⇒\\,y=-4.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(-4)^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446801 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446818 1069 "q-99212" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446818 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers whose sum is $6.$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446818 1072 #uuid "e9c4a6df-1087-507e-b451-15931ae49f9b" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true] [17592186446818 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the three numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nx+y+z=6.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}yz=\\lambda  \\\\ xz=\\lambda  \\\\ xy=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz & =xz \\\\ yz-xz & =0 \\\\ (y-x)z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =6 \\\\ x+x+x & =6 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(2,2,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(2,2,2)=2(2)(2)=8.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,3) & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & <f(2,2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534981 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0bacdbdc-a887-5be8-858f-c82cd078c3e2" 17592186446746 "